<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zubaidah bint Ja'far</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 831</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Zubayda, Umm Ja'far, Amat al-'Aziz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zubaidah bint Ja'far was an Abbasid princess, a granddaughter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Mansur and the wife and double cousin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a>, whom she married in 781–82; their son became the caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-amin">al-Amin</a>. She is remembered above all for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> and pools she built for Muslim pilgrims on the route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, renamed the Darb Zubaidah in her honour. After a drought she had the Zamzam Well deepened and spent over 2 million dinars on the water supply of Mecca, including an aqueduct from the spring of Hunayn, 95 kilometres to the east. She also rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> after an earthquake in 791. Her son was killed by supporters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a> in 813, and she died in 831.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zubaidah's date of birth is unknown, but she was at least a year younger than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a>. Her father, Ja'far, was a brother of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>, and her mother, Salsal, was an elder sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-khayzuran">al-Khayzuran</a>, the second and most powerful wife of al-Mahdi and mother of the caliphs Musa al-Hadi and Harun al-Rashid. Zubaidah was a pet name, meaning little butter ball, given by her grandfather, the caliph al-Mansur; her birth names were Sukhainah or Amat al-'Aziz. She later took the kunya Umm Ja'far. The exploits of her and Harun form part of the basis for The Thousand and One Nights.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Zubaidah married Harun in 781 or 782 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> residence of Muhammad bin Sulayman. Her son Muhammad, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-amin">al-Amin</a>, was born in April 787. She also raised Abdallah, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a>, born in September 786 to Harun and the concubine Marajil, who died soon afterwards. Zubaidah employed staff to manage her properties and business ventures independently of Harun.</p><p>Her household was luxurious: meals were served on gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> plates, she brought in sandals stitched with gems, and she travelled in a palanquin of silver and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebony">ebony</a>. It is said that her palace sounded like a beehive because she employed a hundred women who had memorised the Qur'an.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On her fifth pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> she found a drought had reduced the Zamzam Well to a trickle. She had it deepened and spent over 2 million dinars on the water supply of Mecca and its province, including an aqueduct from the spring of Hunayn, 95 kilometres to the east, and the Spring of Zubayda on the plain of Arafat. Ibn Khallikan records that she told her engineers she would build even were every stroke of a pickax to cost a dinar.</p><p>She also improved the route across nine hundred miles of desert between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, paving it, clearing boulders and building water storage at intervals. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> wrote that every reservoir, pool or well on the road from Mecca to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> was due to her bounty. She rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> after the earthquake of 791. Harun died in March 809, and her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-amin">al-Amin</a> was killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a>'s supporters in 813; al-Ma'mun gave her the same respect as a son. She died on 10 July 831.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zubaidah bint Ja'far” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Abbasid princes married within the family, as Harun al-Rashid married his cousin <b>Zubayda</b>, a granddaughter of al-Mansur, and they governed provinces: Harun’s succession plan of 802 gave al-Ma’mun Khurasan, and al-Mu’tasim governed Egypt and Syria for his brother.</blockquote><a href="/societies/abbasid-caliphate">Social Hierarchy of the Abbasid Caliphate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.32,44.37&amp;year=821" aria-label="Baghdad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Zubaidah bint Ja'far"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6016 4653h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6016 4653h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.32,44.37&amp;year=821">What was here in 821 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
